namespace Linx {

/**

\addtogroup workflow

\section workflow-intro Introduction

Signal and image processing workflows often involve complex sequences or graphs of operations,
often refered to as pipelines or DAGs (Directed Acyclic Graphs) of operations.
Each operation in the pipeline can be a simple or compoud steps.
Often, one would like to wrap the workflow steps with logging or profiling capabilities.
Linx implements `Flow`, a class for chaining and wrapping operations.
The class also helps getting rid of intermediate, temporary variables.

\section workflow-pipeline Pipelines

A workflow is initialized by passing a label and optional step wrapper to the `Flow` constructor, like a logger or profiler.
Then, operations are performed by specifying inputs and transforms.
Outputs are obtained through structured bindings.

Pipelines are the simplest workflows:
They merely chain operations together without any branching.
The following example pipeline is initialized with some timer,
then performs a median filter followed by series of element-wise operations:

```
Linx::TimerLogger logger;
auto [out] = Linx::Flow("Compute noise map", logger)
    .append(in)
    .run(Linx::MedianFilter(strel))
    .transform(Linx::Max(0.), Linx::Add(noise * noise), Linx::Sqrt());
```

Executing this workflow will log the time taken for each step, e.g.:

```
2025-05-28 15:36:00Z | Compute noise map | Input | 0 | 486
2025-05-28 15:36:00Z | Compute noise map | MedianFilter | 15 | 501
2025-05-28 15:36:01Z | Compute noise map | Apply pointwise | 3 | 504
```

\section workflow-states Multi-state workflows

Multi-state workflows allow for more complex operations than pipelines.

*/

}
